93 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507399 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507399 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186507084 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507399 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "fd43d7b1-c262-5c77-be4b-52447e7c391c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507433 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507361 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507433 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143511" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507433 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507433 #edb/ref 1071 "| $x$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $f(x)$ | $0.3$ | $0.5$ | $0.1$ | $0.1$ |\n\nLet ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{80}$ be a random sample of size $n=80$ from a population with the probability mass function given above. Find an approximation for the probability that the sample mean is greater than $0.1.$\n\n*Note: $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution.*" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507433 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "48e8571c-7a4d-574c-8be0-d4e37adb9dc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507433 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case:\n\n- the sample size is \n\n$$\nn=80,\n$$\n\n- the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\mu  & =\\text{E}[X] \\\\  & ={\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{x}_{i}f({x}_{i}) \\\\  & =-1\\cdot 0.3+0\\cdot 0.5+1\\cdot 0.1+2\\cdot 0.1 \\\\  & =-0.3+0+0.1+0.3 \\\\  & =0,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- the population variance is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sigma }^{2} & =\\text{Var}[X] \\\\  & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =((-1)^{2}\\cdot 0.3+{0}^{2}\\cdot 0.5+{1}^{2}\\cdot 0.1+{2}^{2}\\cdot 0.1)-{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(0.3+0+0.1+0.4)-0 \\\\  & =0.8.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(0,\\frac{0.8}{80}) \\\\  & ∼N(0,0.01).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.1).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(\\overset{―}{X}>0.1) & ≈P(Z>\\frac{0.1-0}{\\sqrt{0.01}}) \\\\  & =P(Z>1) \\\\  & =1-Φ(1).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\nP(\\overset{―}{X}>0.1)=1-Φ(1).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507438 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507399 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507438 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143487" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507438 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507438 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{108}$ be a random sample of size $n=108$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =20$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =90.$ Find an approximation for $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.07\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.08\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.09\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0021\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0020\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0019\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0028\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0027\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0026\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-2.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0038\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0037\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0036\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.8\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0344\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0336\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0329\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.7\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0427\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0418\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0409\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.6\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0526\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0516\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.0505\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507438 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4f473f90-fdf2-5352-8207-63a1fa1e5ffa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507438 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=108,\\,\\mu =20,\\,\\sigma =90.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(20,\\frac{{90}^{2}}{108}) \\\\  & ∼N(20,75).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5) & =P(-5<\\overset{―}{X}<5) \\\\  & =P(\\frac{-5-20}{\\sqrt{75}}<Z<\\frac{5-20}{\\sqrt{75}}) \\\\  & =P(-\\frac{5}{\\sqrt{3}}<Z<-\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{3}}) \\\\  & ≈P(-2.89<Z<-1.73) \\\\  & =P(Z<-1.73)-P(Z<-2.89) \\\\  & =Φ(-1.73)-Φ(-2.89).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-2.89)=0.0019, \\\\ Φ(-1.73)=0.0418.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<5) & =Φ(-1.73)-Φ(-2.89) \\\\  & =0.0418-0.0019 \\\\  & =0.0399.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507486 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186507299 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507486 #edb/ref 1069 "q-143491" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507486 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507486 #edb/ref 1071 "Let ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{126}$ be a random sample of size $n=126$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu =5$ and population standard deviation $\\sigma =84.$ Find an approximation for $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5).$\n\n*Hint: Two tables of values of $Φ(z)$ for different values of $z$ are given below, where $Φ(z)$ is the cumulative distribution function for the standard normal distribution. *\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.03\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.04\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.05\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1093\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1075\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1056\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1292\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1271\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1251\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-1.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1515\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1492\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.1469\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"5\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"25%\">$z$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.00\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.01\\,$</th><th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.02\\,$</th>\n</tr><tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.2\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4207\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4168\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4129\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,-0.1\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4602\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4562\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.4522\\,$ </td></tr>\n<tr> <th align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\">$\\,0.0\\,$</th><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5000\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5040\\,$ </td><td align=\"center\"> $\\,0.5080\\,$ </td></tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507486 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b6fde37e-341a-51be-b26a-9707a878cfcb" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507486 #edb/ref 1074 "Suppose that ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a random sample of size $n$ from a population with a population mean $\\mu $ and population standard deviation $\\sigma .$ The central limit theorem (or CLT) states that for sufficiently large $n,$ the distribution of the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ can be approximated as \n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nn=126,\\,\\mu =5,\\,\\sigma =84.\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the CLT, we have that the distribution of the sample mean can be approximated as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{―}{X} & ∼N\\,(\\mu ,\\frac{{\\sigma }^{2}}{n}) \\\\  & ∼N\\,(5,\\frac{{84}^{2}}{126}) \\\\  & ∼N(5,56).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe want to find $P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5).$ So, we convert $\\overset{―}{X}$ to a standard normal random variable by $z$-scoring:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5) & =1-P(|\\overset{―}{X}|<3.5) \\\\  & =1-P(-3.5<\\overset{―}{X}<3.5) \\\\  & =1-P(\\frac{-3.5-5}{\\sqrt{56}}<Z<\\frac{3.5-5}{\\sqrt{56}}) \\\\  & ≈1-P(-1.14<Z<-0.20) \\\\  & =1-(P(Z<-0.20)-P(Z<-1.14)) \\\\  & =1+Φ(-1.14)-Φ(-0.20).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the tables, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Φ(-1.14)=0.1271, \\\\ Φ(-0.20)=0.4207.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(|\\overset{―}{X}|>3.5) & =1+Φ(-1.14)-Φ(-0.20) \\\\  & =1+0.1271-0.4207 \\\\  & =0.7064.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507542 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507542 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "2e4327f0-b411-56a6-82d7-c8367aca26b2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507542 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064474 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507563 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "87a4f78b-19ba-5d81-9385-95bf1e4a05b3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507563 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186507433 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507575 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507563 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507575 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "c6277295-1816-5267-92fd-26924ebecec2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507575 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186427624 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507579 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186507596 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507579 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d7087206-3b5a-5657-b46a-f70f4a9b4817" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186507579 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 1759218650